  "body": "## Finding Description and Impact\n\n### Summary\n\nThe cancellation of withdrawals followed by redelegation using the redelegateWithdrawnHYPE function can lead to systematic buffer depletion. This occurs because funds that were originally included in the buffer via user deposits will be incorrectly redelegated to validators, violating the buffer management logic. Over time, this vulnerability could lead to buffer exhaustion, increasing gas costs, and potential withdrawal delays due to more L1 operations being required. Additionally the L1 SpotDeposit operation attempt is inherently flawed because it assumes the buffered funds are in the L1 Spot Account while they actually reside in the L2 EVM balance.\n\n### Detailed Description\n\nWhen a withdrawal is processed, funds can come from two sources: the protocol's buffer or direct withdrawals from validators through L1 operations. However, when a withdrawal is cancelled and funds are later redelegated, the redelegation process doesn't maintain this distinction. To see why let us take a quick look at the cancel withdrawal function: \n\n````\n    function cancelWithdrawal(address user, uint256 withdrawalId) external onlyRole(MANAGER_ROLE) whenNotPaused {\n        WithdrawalRequest storage request = _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n        require(request.hypeAmount > 0, \"No such withdrawal request\");\n\n        uint256 hypeAmount = request.hypeAmount;\n        uint256 kHYPEAmount = request.kHYPEAmount;\n        uint256 kHYPEFee = request.kHYPEFee;\n\n        // Check kHYPE balances\n        require(kHYPE.balanceOf(address(this)) >= kHYPEAmount + kHYPEFee, \"Insufficient kHYPE balance\");\n\n        // Clear the withdrawal request\n        delete _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n        totalQueuedWithdrawals -= hypeAmount;\n\n        // Return kHYPE tokens to user (including fees)\n        kHYPE.transfer(user, kHYPEAmount + kHYPEFee);\n\n        // Track cancelled amount for future redelegation\n        _cancelledWithdrawalAmount += hypeAmount;\n\n        emit WithdrawalCancelled(user, withdrawalId, hypeAmount, _cancelledWithdrawalAmount);\n    }\n````\n\nNotice how the full amount to withdraw is added to the _cancelledWithdrawalAmount amount. The accumulated _cancelledWithdrawalAmount is then used by the redelegateWithdrawnHYPE to redelegate the withdrawn HYPE that was previously cancelled. \n\nThe redelegateWithdrawnHYPE function uses the OperationType.SpotDeposit when calling the _distributeStake function. \n\n````\nelse if (operationType == OperationType.SpotDeposit) {\n            // For spot deposits, first move from spot balance to staking balance\n            uint256 truncatedAmount = _convertTo8Decimals(amount, false);\n            require(truncatedAmount <= type(uint64).max, \"Amount exceeds uint64 max\");\n\n            // 1. First move from spot balance to staking balance using cDeposit\n            l1Write.sendCDeposit(uint64(truncatedAmount));\n\n            // 2. Queue the delegation operation (8 decimals)\n            _queueL1Operation(validator, truncatedAmount, OperationType.RebalanceDeposit);\n        }\n````\n\nBut notice that Spot Deposit operations sends all funds directly for delegation and ignores all buffer logic. This is in contrast to OperationType.UserDeposit operations. Let us see how this can develop:\n\n\nInitial Setup:\n\nCurrent buffer: 10 HYPE\n1000 HYPE delegated to validators\n\n\n\n1. User Deposits 50 HYPE, 10 kept in EVM buffer, and 40 delegated to stakers on L1.\n2. User requests to withdraw 50 HYPE via queueWithdrawal. 10 HYPE should come from from buffer (reducing it to 0), 40 HYPE should come from validators via L1 operation.\n6. Manager Cancels Withdrawal by calling cancelWithdrawal.\n7. _cancelledWithdrawalAmount increases by 50 HYPE.\n8. Manager calls redelegateWithdrawnHYPE to redelegate cancelled withdrawals.\n9. All 50 HYPE is redelegated using OperationType.SpotDeposit from L1 spot account to L1 staking balance.\n9. Buffer remains at 0 HYPE, all 50 HYPE sent back to validators\n10. 10 HYPE that should have remained in EVM buffer is now with validators instead.\n\n\nConsequence of this buffer depletion over time is that more validator L1 operations will be required for withdrawals.\n\nAdditionally, the spotDeposit attempts to transfer the buffered assets without transferring them first from EVM to L1 Spot, causing additional problems on L2.\n\nTo clarify, recall that when the user makes initial deposit, HYPE is moved from EVM to spot balance :\n\n````\n            // For user deposits, move HYPE from EVM to spot balance\n            // 1. Move HYPE from EVM to spot balance on L1 by sending directly to L1 address\n            (bool success, ) = payable(L1_HYPE_CONTRACT).call{value: amount}(\"\");\n            require(success, \"Failed to send HYPE to L1\");\n````\n\nHowever, this amount moved does not include buffered funds. The SpotDeposit transaction then simply assumes the buffered funds are already in spot and attempts transfers them to L1 staking balance directly.\n\nThis also lends support to the fact that sending buffered assets in EVM to L1 is not intentional.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nOne potential fix is to add the buffering logic in the userDposit block  and add it to the SpotDeposit block:\n\n````\n            uint256 currentBuffer = hypeBuffer;\n            uint256 target = targetBuffer;\n            if (amount > 0 && currentBuffer < target) {\n                uint256 bufferSpace = target - currentBuffer;\n                uint256 amountToBuffer = Math.min(amount, bufferSpace);\n                hypeBuffer = currentBuffer + amountToBuffer;\n                amount -= amountToBuffer;\n\n                emit BufferIncreased(amountToBuffer, hypeBuffer);\n            }\n\n            // If no amount left after buffer, return\n            if (amount == 0) {\n                return;\n            }\n\n            // Ensure amount after buffer is still divisible by 1e10\n            uint256 remainder = amount % 1e10;\n            if (remainder > 0) {\n                // Add the remainder to the buffer\n                hypeBuffer += remainder;\n                amount -= remainder;\n                /// @dev It will retain those decimals in this contract, providing exit liquidity.\n\n                // If all amount went to buffer, return\n                if (amount == 0) {\n                    return;\n                }\n            }\n````\n\nRun the POC again, it should fail with \"Buffer should still be depleted after redelegation\"",
  "proof_of_concept": "Add this test to the StakingManager.t.sol:\n\n````\n    function test_BufferDepletionDuringRedelegation() public {\n        // Setup validator and delegation\n        vm.startPrank(manager);\n        validatorManager.activateValidator(validator);\n        validatorManager.setDelegation(address(stakingManager), validator);\n        // Set higher max stake amount\n        stakingManager.setMaxStakeAmount(2 ether);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Set target buffer to 1 ETH\n        vm.prank(manager);\n        stakingManager.setTargetBuffer(1 ether);\n\n        // User stakes 2 ETH\n        vm.deal(user, 2 ether);\n        vm.prank(user);\n        stakingManager.stake{value: 2 ether}();\n\n        // Verify buffer is filled\n        assertEq(stakingManager.hypeBuffer(), 1 ether, \"Buffer should be filled\");\n\n        // Deposit ETH to contract for withdrawal\n        vm.deal(address(stakingManager), 2 ether);\n\n        // User queues withdrawal of 1.5 ETH\n        vm.prank(user);\n        kHYPE.approve(address(stakingManager), 1.5 ether);\n        vm.prank(user);\n        stakingManager.queueWithdrawal(1.5 ether);\n\n        // Verify buffer is depleted (1 ETH from buffer, 0.5 ETH from validator)\n        assertEq(stakingManager.hypeBuffer(), 0, \"Buffer should be depleted\");\n\n        // Manager cancels withdrawal\n        vm.prank(manager);\n        stakingManager.cancelWithdrawal(user, 0);\n\n        // Redelegate the cancelled amount\n        vm.prank(manager);\n        stakingManager.redelegateWithdrawnHYPE();\n\n        // Verify buffer is still depleted\n        assertEq(stakingManager.hypeBuffer(), 0, \"Buffer should still be depleted after redelegation\");\n    }\n````\n\nIt should pass, showing that buffer remains depleted after redelegation.",
